## 🎯 The Problem

Scientific datasets power critical decisions in environmental monitoring, healthcare, and beyond. Yet most data quality approaches remain either purely theoretical or narrowly domain-specific.

**Research Question**:  
> *How can we quantify, score, and monitor data quality using a reproducible, statistically-grounded, multi-dimensional framework?*

**Solution**: A literature-backed four-dimension scoring system implemented in a fully reproducible Python/Jupyter pipeline.

---

## 🧱 The Framework — Four Dimensions, One Score

Each dimension yields a normalized [0–1] score. Composite **DQI** uses equal weighting (conservative, extensible).

| Dimension      | Measures                          | Formula                          | Literature Basis              | Score    |
|----------------|-----------------------------------|----------------------------------|-------------------------------|----------|
| **Completeness** | Expected values present?         | `1 − (missing / total)`         | Pipino et al. (2002)         | **0.930** ✅ |
| **Consistency**  | Values conform to rules?         | `1 − (violations / checks)`     | Batini & Scannapieco (2016)  | **0.978** ✅ |
| **Accuracy**     | Closeness to ground truth?       | `1 − (MAE / range)` normalized  | Heinrich et al. (2018)       | **0.824** ✅ |
| **Timeliness**   | How fresh is the data?           | `e^(−0.01 × age_days)`          | Batini & Scannapieco (2016)  | **0.626** ❌ |
| **Composite DQI**| Equal-weighted average           | —                                | Wang & Strong (1996)         | **0.840**   |

